in 2013 I bought the limited edition of  SimCity and although it took me a few  days to finally get into a server once I  did I was really enjoying it well that  was right up until my fire engines  glitched out and decided to drive round  and round in circles while half my town  burned to the ground I really liked city  building games and at the time I was  happy to take SimCity for what it was  and with all its faults right up until  my game broke since there wasn't much  else like it now that's changed with the  release of cities skylines a game that  is quite simply everything that SimCity  2013 should have been at a glance city  skylines looks a lot like SimCity in  fact if it wasn't so good we'd probably  be calling it a clone or direct ripoff  but developer colossal order have  seriously proved themselves but what is  it that makes it so good  right off the bat skylines is much  cheaper than SimCity costing just 30  bucks at launch and seemingly without  sacrificing much at all it also offers  an online mode from the get-go  whereas EA gave gamers the runaround by  saying they needed SimCity to be online  before eventually caving and adding an  offline mode although not before  alienating a lot of their players  skyline seems like it has a lot more  flexibility and room to grow compared to  SimCity thanks to an already thriving  mod community and more comprehensive  gameplay features which cater to those  of you who love micromanaging beyond  that city skyline simply feels more  alive and true to its promise of  simulating city life than SimCity in  skylines your citizens aren't weirdos  who go to work in a different place  every day and return to the home that's  closest to them like in SimCity they  have their own personal names  occupations homes cars and feelings okay  sometimes your citizens clip into cars  or a crosswalk that you can actually  watch the little dudes go about their  lives which is special in such an  expansive city builder they live and  die you can follow their lives from  childhood right up until the graveyard  hearse comes to pick up their corpse  they graduate from different levels of  school get jobs based on their level of  education and move out of their parents  house so long as the city you've built  lets them have all those opportunities  of course but it's not just about  building skylines lets you manage and  have control over your cities by  allocating districts and applying a  number of policies concerning lifestyle  services taxation and City Planning  these cover everything from compulsory  recycling and the recreational use of  weed to taxing the rich and banning pets  in order to keep your houses clean  policies can be applied to the town as a  whole or simply to districts and  everything you do causes a butterfly  effect of pros and cons to the rest of  your city on top of that the  possibilities for expanded gameplay  features are huge thanks to well  integrated mods and enormous map sizes  unlike other city builders skyline seems  happy to have you build a great big town  and keep on expanding although you start  on a plot of land whose size is similar  to the last Sim City you're pretty  quickly given the opportunity to buy  extra plots on all sides so you'll find  you're never short of room or unable to  make the city that you can see in your  head I mean if somebody can make grand  theft auto 5 Los Santos you can be  reassured that there aren't a lot of  size restrictions while the game might  not feature every city related item you  can dream of there is the constant  promise of new stuff thanks to the  in-game asset and map editor Steam  Workshop integration and the huge  community that's already there that said  city skylines doesn't have that  excellent Maxis soundtrack that  underpinned SimCity and like any good  city planning game you will have to fail  at least once until you can create a  town that doesn't just slowly burn money  until all of your citizens are dead or  weeping City skylines has a lot of  exciting potential and in just a week  the game has sold over 500,000 copies it  manages to be both accessible and  incredibly complex depending on how long  you've been playing through a map and  whether you're a fan of digging into the  specifics of  judgment that's just my opinion though  so be sure to let me know down in that  comment section what you think of cities  skylines and how you reckon it stacks up  to SimCity I'll catch you again next  week
 

